 "company_background": "Cottonwood Creek Animal Hospital was founded in 1998 by Dr. Harold Brightwater and purchased by Dr. Mehra-Castillo in 2019 with an SBA 7(a) loan from this bank, which has always been paid as agreed. The hospital is a small-animal general practice with limited exotics, open six days a week, with urgent-care hours until 9 p.m. on weekdays. It employs four veterinarians (the owner plus three associates), eleven credentialed technicians, six client-service staff and a practice manager. Active client count was 9,860 at June 2026, with a 4.8-star average across 1,140 online reviews. The practice participates in two pet-insurance direct-pay programs and launched a wellness-plan membership in 2024 that now has 1,320 enrolled pets paying monthly. The second surgical suite is intended to bring orthopedic and soft-tissue referrals in-house that are currently sent to a specialty center 70 miles away; the owner estimates 18-25 additional procedures per month once a boarded surgeon is contracted two days a week. The building has been leased since 2004; the landlord, a retired physician, offered it for sale at $780,000 after the lease came up for renewal. The roof was replaced in 2021 and the HVAC in 2023. Phase I environmental screening found no recognized environmental conditions; the parcel is in flood zone X.",
 "industry_notes": "Companion-animal spending in the region has grown faster than inflation since 2020, driven by higher pet ownership and more frequent use of diagnostics. The main operating constraint across the industry is staffing: veterinarian and technician vacancies remain high and wage inflation for credentialed technicians ran about 7% last year. Corporate consolidators have bought three of the eleven practices within 20 miles since 2022; two of those raised exam fees by more than 15%, which the owner believes has pushed clients toward independent practices. Pet-insurance penetration remains low but is rising, which tends to support higher average invoices.",
